[What is AI email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): AI email marketing is the use of AI to write email content and decide when, how, and to whom each message is sent, based on what customers actually do rather than fixed rules. It covers two things: generative AI that creates the content, and AI-driven optimization that handles timing, audience, and message selection per person.

[How do you use AI in email marketing?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): You use AI in email marketing for five main jobs: generating and testing content variants, optimizing send time per individual, segmenting by live behavior, selecting the next best message through decisioning, and protecting deliverability with AI-assisted monitoring. Each one replaces manual setup with a decision pulled from how each customer behaves.

[What is the difference between generative AI and AI optimization in email?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): Generative AI creates the email, drafting subject lines, body copy, and creative variants at volume. AI optimization decides what happens with them, choosing the send time, the audience, and the message each person receives. One produces the range of options. The other selects which option goes to whom.

[What can AI do for email marketing that automation cannot?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): Automation repeats fixed if-then logic a marketer builds once. AI decides from behavior as it happens, so the logic updates itself. It can weigh how each person engaged, choose the message and timing for them individually, and hold a send entirely, rather than treating everyone in a flow the same way.

[Which email platforms have the best AI?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-marketing): The best AI depends on what you need. Platforms with built-in AI like Mailchimp, Klaviyo, and Salesforce Marketing Cloud suit single-channel email. Cross-channel platforms like Iterable, MoEngage, and Braze go further, making AI decisions across email, push, SMS, and more from one customer profile.

[What is AI email personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): AI email personalization uses machine learning to tailor email content, offers, subject lines, and send timing to each recipient based on their behavior and preferences. It goes beyond inserting a name, generating and selecting the most relevant content per person and updating as new data arrives.

[How does AI email personalization work?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): It works in two steps: generative AI creates on-brand content variants (subject lines, copy, recommendations), and decisioning models select which variant, offer, and send time each recipient receives based on observed behavior. Machine learning refines those choices continuously as engagement data accumulates.

[How is AI email personalization different from basic personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): Basic personalization inserts known fields like a first name or last purchase. AI email personalization analyzes behavioral and contextual data to tailor content, timing, and offers to the individual, and adapts automatically over time, making messages far more relevant than static merge tags or segment-level rules.

[What data does AI email personalization use?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): It draws on first-party behavioral data (opens, clicks, purchases, browsing), profile attributes, lifecycle stage, and real-time context like location or time. Unified in one customer profile, this data lets models predict intent and choose the most relevant content and send time for each recipient.

[Does AI email personalization replace marketers?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ai-email-personalization): No. AI handles data analysis, content generation, and per-recipient selection at scale, but marketers set strategy, guardrails, and brand voice and review output for quality. The best results come from AI doing the heavy lifting while humans keep messaging on-brand and on-strategy.

[What is AI-powered eCommerce personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-personalization-guide): AI-powered eCommerce personalization refers to the use of machine learning to tailor content, recommendations, timing, and channels in real time. The approach learns from behavior across web, app, email, push, SMS, and in-app to keep experiences relevant.

[How can brands personalize the shopping experience in real time?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-personalization-guide): Brands personalize the shopping experience in real time by streaming behavioral data into unified profiles, triggering journeys based on fresh signals, and using predictive models to select the next message or offer. This turns live context into timely, helpful interactions.

[What are examples of effective personalization in eCommerce?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-personalization-guide): Examples of effective personalization in eCommerce include abandoned-cart recovery with dynamic content, predictive offer selection tailored to each shopper, and post-purchase journeys with care tips and thoughtful recommendations.

[How does personalization improve conversions and retention?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-personalization-guide): Personalization improves conversions and retention by making interactions more relevant, which increases response, repeat purchases, and loyalty. Adding a second messaging channel, for example, is associated with 4.5X gains in purchases per user according to the Braze 2025 Customer Engagement Review report.

[What’s the difference between basic and predictive personalization?](https://www.braze.com/resources/articles/ecommerce-personalization-guide): The difference between basic and predictive personalization is that basic relies on static rules and simple attributes, while predictive uses models to anticipate intent and adapt content, timing, and channel for each individual. Predictive approaches keep learning as new behavior appears.

